; chirality scaffold port floor: the category C boundary, declared in source.
;
; porttype introduces an opaque linear atom: the kernel learns nothing about
; it except that it exists and that B-ness lives in the type, so a value of
; it (or anything transitively holding one) can only ever be bound with
; quantity 1. Every crossing below is an extern: its type is owned here, the
; host implementation is bound at link time, and the checker holds every
; caller to move-only threading.
;
; H11: the declarations themselves now live in nine registries under
; scaffold/lib/ports/, one per concern. This file is the FAÇADE -- importing
; "ports" still names the whole floor, so every existing importer is
; unchanged. Import a single registry instead when you want the smaller
; naming surface:
;
;   ports/fd       Fd cap + adopt-fd/fd-close + read/write-fd/fcntl
;   ports/sock     Sock/LSock + the socket crossings + their result sums
;   ports/pool     sized shared buffers (Pool n) [needs ports/fd]
;   ports/file     open-rw / open-create -- a NAME becomes a descriptor
;   ports/pty      Pty cap, acquisition, child wiring, spawn-in-pty
;   ports/tty      line-discipline ioctls + read-key + Key/KeySeq/Keymap
;   ports/stdio    print / put / trace
;   ports/clock    Clock, Timer, Env caps and their result sums
;   ports/process  exit / halt / signal / mmap / mprotect
;
; What that buys is a NAMING boundary -- what a module is allowed to name --
; and nothing else. It is worth exactly 0 bytes at runtime: `native-lib ++
; link-lib` is appended unconditionally (compile-emit.chiral:189), so a program
; importing one registry and a program importing all nine emit the same size.

(import "prelude/prelude")
(import "ports/fd")
(import "ports/sock")
(import "ports/pool")
(import "ports/file")
(import "ports/pty")
(import "ports/tty")
(import "ports/stdio")
(import "ports/clock")
(import "ports/process")

; E160: the coordinate that this file's opening line used to state in prose.
;
; NO PORT-SET CLAIM, and that is a FINDING rather than an omission. The port-set
; claim is decided against the crossings a module's OWN defs reach, and since
; H11 moved the declarations into the nine registries above, this file defines
; no def at all -- it is a FAÇADE. E160 made this the awkward case: neither
; `(crosses)` nor `(pure)` was writable without lying, so the axis was left
; unstated. E161 dissolves it rather than working around it. The axis is DERIVED
; and its sense is DECLARED as "what this module binds outward", so a façade's
; `crossings` is EMPTY and empty is the true reading, not a vacuous one: the
; crossings are real and they live one import down, in ports/fd, ports/sock and
; the rest, on those modules' own sheets. The category letter is still this
; file's to state: importing "ports" names the category C floor.
(module ports/ports (cat C) (alt upper))

; scriba — whitelisted through B1 build source.
; The one declaration that is not a port: `Port` is scriba's puffer HANDLE
; placeholder (TUI/scriba/puffer.chiral), a nullary data type with no crossing
; behind it. It fits none of the nine registries, and inventing a tenth for a
; single scriba placeholder would be worse than leaving it on the façade until
; the puffer handle gets a real type.
(data Port () (port))
